  - q: "How much does cured in place pipe cost per foot?"
    a: "The per-foot section above works it out from completed residential jobs: lining came in at roughly half the per-foot cost of the excavation bids on the same pipes, with the exact ranges shown there and updated as new projects are posted. Short runs and small diameters cost more per foot because set-up, cleaning and reinstatement are fixed costs spread over fewer feet."
  - q: "Why is CIPP cheaper than digging when the materials cost more?"
    a: "The liner and resin do cost more per foot than a stick of PVC. What they eliminate is everything around the trench: saw-cutting and hauling concrete, excavating and shoring, backfill, compaction, re-pouring a driveway or slab, re-landscaping, traffic control and the days of labor all of that takes. On most lots the restoration is the biggest line item in a replacement bid, and lining removes it."
  - q: "Does homeowners insurance pay for sewer lining?"
    a: "Standard policies usually exclude wear, root damage and gradual deterioration, which is what most failed sewers are. Some carriers sell a service-line endorsement that covers the lateral; check your declarations page. A few utilities and cities also run lateral assistance or low-interest loan programs. Your contractor's written estimate is what any of those will ask for."
  - q: "What is a fair deposit and payment schedule?"
    a: "Common practice for a one-day residential lining job is a modest deposit at scheduling and the balance on completion, after you have watched the final camera recording. Be cautious of anyone asking for most of the money up front on a job that will be finished the same day it starts."
  - q: "Is a cheaper lining quote a red flag?"
    a: "Not by itself, but a quote far below the others on the same pipe usually means something was left out: cleaning, a reinstatement, a cleanout installation, or a thinner liner than the pipe calls for. Ask every bidder to state the liner and resin system, the design thickness, the number of reinstatements included and the warranty in writing, then compare."
  - q: "Can I get a cost estimate without a camera inspection?"
    a: "You can get a ballpark by the foot, but no responsible installer will give a firm price without seeing the pipe. The inspection tells them the length, the diameter, how many connections to reopen, how much cleaning is needed and whether any section has to be excavated first. Many installers on this site waive or credit the inspection fee when you proceed with the work."
