<h2>What moves the price</h2>
<h3>Up</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Length and diameter.</strong> More liner, more resin, longer cure. A 6-inch line costs more per foot than a 4-inch.</li>
  <li><strong>Branch connections.</strong> Each one the liner covers has to be reopened with a robotic cutter. A main-line lateral with one connection is simple; a building drain with six is a different job.</li>
  <li><strong>Heavy cleaning.</strong> Cast iron with thick scale, dense roots or grease can add hours of descaling before the liner can go in.</li>
  <li><strong>No access.</strong> If there is no cleanout, the crew installs one, which usually means a small excavation.</li>
  <li><strong>Pre-repairs.</strong> A collapsed or badly offset section has to be fixed by a point excavation before lining; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/repair">cured in place pipe repair</a> page explains when that is unavoidable.</li>
  <li><strong>Size changes and bends.</strong> A transition liner that steps from 4-inch to 6-inch, or a run with several 45-degree bends, takes more engineering and more care.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Down</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Existing cleanouts</strong> at both ends.</li>
  <li><strong>Clay or PVC host pipe</strong> that cleans quickly.</li>
  <li><strong>Longer straight runs</strong>, which spread the fixed costs.</li>
  <li><strong>Bundling</strong> the lateral with the under-slab drains in one mobilization.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What a replacement bid hides</h2>
<p>A dig-and-replace quote for a residential sewer typically includes excavation, pipe, bedding, backfill and compaction. What it often does not spell out, or lists as "by others," is the restoration: the driveway section, the sidewalk the city requires a permit and a licensed concrete contractor to replace, the mature tree whose roots were the problem in the first place, the irrigation lines, the slab inside the house and the flooring on top of it. When you compare a lining quote to a replacement quote, add those back in. That is the arithmetic behind the table above.</p>

<h2>How to compare lining quotes with each other</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Ask each bidder for the <strong>liner and resin system</strong> by brand and the <strong>design thickness</strong>. Thinner is cheaper and sometimes fine, but you should know.</li>
  <li>Confirm the quote includes <strong>cleaning, all reinstatements and the final camera recording</strong>, and whether a cleanout installation is in or out.</li>
  <li>Ask what the <strong>warranty</strong> covers and for how long, in writing. Manufacturer material warranties and the installer's workmanship warranty are two different documents.</li>
  <li>Ask about their <strong>training on the specific system</strong>; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page lists what to look for.</li>
  <li>Ask for the <strong>cure method</strong> (hot water, steam or UV) and how they verify cure. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">installation page</a> explains why that matters to the finished pipe.</li>
</ol>
